```

However, this approach doesn't seem to work as expected. The `StyledContainer` doesn't seem to affect the layout of the `Container` component.

To make it work with TailwindCSS, you should use the `@apply` directive to apply Tailwind classes instead of defining your own styles. Here's how you can do it:

```jsx
import React from 'react';
import { Container, Row, Col } from 'reactstrap';

const MyLayout = ({ variant, children }) => {
  return (
    <div className={`w-full ${variant === 'centered' ? 'flex justify-center items-center' : ''}`}>
      <Container>
        <Row>
          <Col>{children}</Col>
        </Row>
      </Container>
    </div>
  );
};

export default MyLayout;
```

In this example, the `MyLayout` component accepts a `variant` prop, which can be either 'full-width' or 'centered'. If the `variant` is 'centered', it applies the Tailwind classes `flex`, `justify-center`, and `items-center` to the container. If the `variant` is not 'centered', no classes are applied.

This way, you can use TailwindCSS classes directly in your React components, and you don't need to use `styled-components` or any other CSS-in-JS library.

