In React, you can pass properties directly to a component or use `React.cloneElement` when you want to modify the existing element's props or add new children to the existing element.

Here's a brief comparison:

1. Passing properties directly:

```jsx
function MyComponent(props) {
  return <div {...props}>{props.children}</div>;
}

// Usage
<MyComponent className="my-class" />
```

In this example, you're passing the `className` property directly to the `MyComponent` component. This is the most common way to pass properties to a component.

2. Using `React.cloneElement`:

```jsx
function MyComponent(props) {
  return (
    <div>
      {React.Children.map(props.children, (child, index) =>
        React.cloneElement(child, { key: index })
      )}
    </div>
  );
}

// Usage
<MyComponent>
  <div className="child1" />
  <div className="child2" />
</MyComponent>
